/T (Stage 1: Observation Without Assumptions The team's first intervention was to observe. We visited Cafe Adda repeatedly during peak hours, lunch, and evening, without preconceptions, watching how the crowd behaved, how staff moved, where congestion formed, and at what point the service model visibly broke down. It became immediately clear that the problem was not the quality of food or even the speed of individual staff, but the absence of any structural mechanism to manage simultaneous demand.)
/E (Stage 1: Observation Without Assumptions The team's first intervention was to observe. We visited Cafe Adda repeatedly during peak hours, lunch, and evening, without preconceptions, watching how the crowd behaved, how staff moved, where congestion formed, and at what point the service model visibly broke down. It became immediately clear that the problem was not the quality of food or even the speed of individual staff, but the absence of any structural mechanism to manage simultaneous demand.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
